id: k8s.service
kind: kubernetes
title: Service
aliases: svc, service discovery object
tags: networking, discovery
questions:
  - how does a Pod get a stable address?
invariant: A Service is a stable virtual IP and DNS name that load-balances to the Pods currently matching its selector.
answer_30s: A Service gives a fixed name and cluster IP in front of a changing set of Pod endpoints, selected by labels.
answer_2m: |
  Endpoints come from the selector: the endpoints controller adds a Pod when it
  is Ready and matching, and removes it otherwise. That is the join between
  probes and traffic -- which is why a wrong readiness probe looks like a
  networking bug. kube-proxy (or an eBPF data path) implements the virtual IP
  on each node, so the ClusterIP is reachable from inside the cluster only.
when_to_use:
  - any workload that other workloads must reach by a stable name
commands:
  - kubectl get svc -A => which services exist, their type and cluster IP
  - kubectl get endpoints api => is the endpoint list empty, i.e. no Pod is Ready
  - kubectl describe svc/api => selector, ports and which endpoints back it
failure_modes:
  - selector does not match the Pod labels, so endpoints stay empty
  - targetPort wrong, so connections are refused
  - Pods unready (probe or crash) so they are removed from the endpoints
follow_ups:
  - what is the difference between port, targetPort and nodePort?
related:
  - k8s.endpoints
  - k8s.probe.readiness
  - k8s.dns-service-discovery
  - k8s.service.clusterip
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/
